   "text": "\n\n## How a Hash Table Handles Collisions\n\nA hash table maps keys to values using a hash function that converts a key into an index in an array. Collisions occur when two different keys produce the same index. Since hash functions map a large key space into a smaller array, collisions are inevitable \u2014 the design question is how to resolve them efficiently.\n\n### The Two Main Strategies\n\n**1. Chaining** \u2014 Each array slot holds a linked list (or another collection) of all entries that hash to that index. Insertion and lookup degrade gracefully: the cost is proportional to the number of entries in that particular bucket. Chaining is simple and handles high load factors well, but it adds pointer overhead and can hurt cache locality.\n\n**2. Open Addressing** \u2014 All entries live directly in the array. When a collision is detected, the algorithm probes for the next available slot using a deterministic sequence. The three classic probing schemes are:\n\n- **Linear probing:** check index `i+1`, `i+2`, `i+3`, \u2026 Simple but suffers from *primary clustering* \u2014 long runs of occupied slots form, making subsequent probes scan many consecutive entries.\n- **Quadratic probing:** check `i+1\u00b2`, `i"
