   "text": "### TCP Three-Way Handshake and SYN Packet Loss\n\nThe TCP three-way handshake is the process by which a client and server establish a connection. Below, we will illustrate this process and what happens when a SYN packet is lost using three images.\n\n#### 1. Normal Three-Way Handshake\n\nIn a normal connection, the client first sends a SYN (synchronize) packet to the server. The server responds with a SYN-ACK (synchronize-acknowledge) packet. Finally, the client sends an ACK (acknowledge) packet to confirm the connection.\n\n**Step 1: Client sends SYN**\n\nThe client initiates the connection by sending a SYN packet to the server. This packet contains the client's initial sequence number (ISN).\n\n**Step 2: Server responds with SYN-ACK**\n\nUpon receiving the SYN packet, the server sends a SYN-ACK packet back to the client. This packet contains the server's ISN and an acknowledgment number (ACK) for the client's ISN.\n\n**Step 3: Client sends ACK**\n\nFinally, the client sends an ACK packet to the server, acknowledging the server's ISN. At this point, the connection is established, and data transfer can begin.\n\n"
